    "body": "A squirrel races through an obstacle course, a machine launches a clever surprise, and viewers wonder how it all works. These scenes sound like cartoons, but engineer Mark Rober uses real inventions to explain science. Before becoming widely known for online experiments, he worked at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory. His career connects two activities that may seem different: designing equipment for space exploration and teaching people through entertaining videos. Both require careful testing and the ability to learn when something does not work the first time.\n\n## Engineering for another planet\n\nMark studied mechanical engineering, a field focused on how objects move, fit, and work together. He spent nine years at NASA, including seven years working on the Curiosity rover project. Curiosity is a robot that landed on Mars in 2012 to study the planet's surface. A rover cannot visit a repair shop when a wheel or other part breaks. Engineers must test equipment on Earth while imagining the harsh temperatures, dust, and distance it will face millions of miles away. That makes planning and teamwork especially important.\n\n## A camera changes the classroom\n\nMark started posting videos while he still had an engineering job. One early project used tablet screens to create the illusion of a person with a hole through their body for a costume. The trick was not actual magic. It combined cameras, screens, and careful positioning. Over time, his videos grew into large demonstrations with unexpected results. Their playful tone helps viewers ask serious questions: why did the device move that way, and what scientific principle explains what happened?\n\n## The squirrel obstacle course\n\nIn a popular set of videos, Mark designed obstacle courses for squirrels in his backyard. The animals climbed, jumped, and learned how to navigate barriers to reach food. It would be easy to view the video only as a contest between a human inventor and clever animals. But the more interesting question is how animals learn through repeated attempts. A squirrel may make a mistake, then use the experience to try a different path. Watching this process can reveal as much about behavior as it does about machines.\n\n## Learning through making\n\nMark later created CrunchLabs, a company that develops hands-on engineering projects. Its goal is to help people practice thinking like engineers. A project begins with a problem, such as moving an object without touching it. Builders sketch ideas, choose materials, make prototypes, and observe what happens. A prototype is an early version used for testing. If the first version fails, its failure provides information about what to change. This is normal in engineering, not a reason to stop experimenting.\n\n## Make a prediction first\n\nTry a safe investigation with a paper airplane. Fold two designs, predict which will travel farther, then throw both from the same place with similar force. Measure the distances and repeat the test. You may discover your prediction was wrong. That is useful data, not an embarrassment. Mark Rober's work encourages curiosity, but the most important part is not the enormous machines in his videos. It is the habit of asking a question, building a test, and learning from evidence.",
