    "body": "A regular video can be watched long after it is recorded. A livestream is different: viewers and a creator may be present at the same moment. Imane Anys, known online as Pokimane, became one of the best-known game streamers by sharing play sessions and conversations with a large audience. Viewers might see a surprising move in a game and respond immediately through a chat box. The creator can then react to the messages while continuing to play. That makes streaming feel more like a live event than a finished television show.\n\n## Finding a voice on camera\n\nImane was born in Morocco and grew up in Canada. She enjoyed games and began streaming while studying. Streaming takes several skills at once. A person has to understand the game, explain what is happening, watch technical equipment, and respond to viewers without becoming too distracted. If the microphone stops working, the creator has to fix the problem. If a chat message is unkind, the creator must decide how to handle it. The audience sees entertainment, but the streamer is managing a busy communication task.\n\n## Games and conversations\n\nPokimane became especially associated with games such as League of Legends and Fortnite. The two games have different goals and styles, yet both can create moments that are interesting to watch. Sometimes the appeal is skillful play; at other times it is the creator's reaction or discussion with friends. In this format, personality matters. Viewers may return because they feel familiar with the streamer's jokes, opinions about games, and way of talking. That feeling can be enjoyable, but the relationship still takes place through a screen.\n\n## The importance of boundaries\n\nPublic attention can bring excitement and difficulty. A popular streamer may receive thousands of messages, including criticism or demands for personal information. Responsible creators have to protect privacy and decide what parts of their lives to keep offline. Viewers also have responsibilities. Someone can enjoy a creator's videos without expecting a personal friendship, demanding a response, or sharing private details. This kind of boundary helps make an online community safer for everyone.\n\n## How a chat becomes a community\n\nMany streams use moderators, people who help enforce rules in chat. Rules may limit insults, spam, or personal information. A respectful chat gives more people a chance to participate. The same principles apply to classroom discussions: listen, use kind words, and remember that other people may see a situation differently. A stream with a large audience can become confusing without clear expectations and people helping keep the conversation organized.\n\n## Create your own streaming rules\n\nImagine your class is hosting a safe, private livestream about a game or book. Write three chat rules and explain why each one matters. Perhaps one rule protects people's names, another encourages respectful disagreement, and a third keeps comments connected to the topic. Then think about who would help enforce the rules. Pokimane's story is about games, but it is also about communication, attention, and the responsibility that comes with having an audience.",
